{% extends "voc2_en.j2" %}

{% block summary %}
<p>OSLO-Person focuses on describing a natural person, i.e. a physical person and not an organization. Natural persons have their own legal personality, which is not artificial as with legal persons.</p><p>The vocabulary is an extension of existing RDF vocabularies about people. After all, these are only intended to identify a person (e.g. the <a class="c2" href="https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/node/42440">ISA Core Person Vocabulary</a> that models name, gender, date of birth, etc.) or they are designed for specific applications (e.g. <a class="c2" href="http://xmlns.com/foaf/spec/">FOAF</a> that focuses mainly on relationships between persons). </p><p>What was still missing is a vocabulary for the kind of information that is typically kept in a national population register. This concerns data with civil law implications such as parentage, birth, marriage. The entry of such information in a register confirms its official nature and guarantees the person concerned the rights and obligations attaching to it. </p><p>The OSLO-Person vocabulary is more extensive than e.g. ISA Core Person, but not modeled on a specific national register. An attempt was made to select the civil law characteristics that appear in all national population registers. In order to describe the details of a specific population register, the user must expand the data model. </p><p>Compared to ISA Core Person, personal relationships of different kinds are added: parentage, marriage, cohabitation, custody and family relationships. The Family entity has been added and also Marital Status and Nationality. Also new is the elaboration of the existing relationship in ISA Core Person between a person and a jurisdiction. It concerns the relationships between citizenship and residency, in which OSLO-Persoon can distinguish between citizens and foreigners and between residents and non-residents. A person's place of residence and his more specialized form of domicile (with corresponding official address) can now also be exchanged. The address provided by ISA is part of contact info. </p><p>Data on property law, criminal law or social law data are not included in the OSLO Personal vocabulary.</p>	
{% endblock %}

